[size=6][b]Turret Behaviour Menu (Resurrected)[/b][/size]

A right-click-menu mod for X4: Foundations that lets you set turret behaviour in bulk — across fleets, per turret size, per turret type — and copy a ship's turret configuration onto other ships in one click.

Original design and turret-walking logic by [b]Berserk Knight[/b] (2019). This is a port to current X4 on top of [b]kuertee UI Extensions[/b], with a few additions on top.

[line]

[size=4][b]Game version compatibility[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][i] 9.00 release[/i] - [b]supported[/b][/*]
[*][i] 9.00 betas and release candidates[/i] - [b]supported[/b][/*]
[*][i] 8.00 release[/i] - [b]supported[/b][/*]
[/list]

[line]

[size=4][b]Dependencies[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]kuertee UI Extensions and HUD[/b] ([url=https://www.nexusmods.com/x4foundations/mods/552]link[/url]) — provides the patched interact menu and its callback API. The whole UI integration runs through it.
[*][b]SirNukes Mod Support APIs[/b] ([url=https://www.nexusmods.com/x4foundations/mods/503]link[/url]) - provides the Lua loader, interact-menu API, and simple menu/options helpers.[/*]
[/list]

Both are hard dependencies. The mod will not load without them.

Please, [b]make sure to use the latest Kuertee's UI Extensions mod available for your game version[/b]. If it was not installed previously and the protected ui mode was "on", you would have to disable the protected ui mode first. To do that, first you would have to disable all ui mods, including Kuertee's UI Extensions, then reload the game, then turn off the protected mode, reload the game again, turn on all the mods you like, reload the game again... Then you can play! :)

[line]

[size=4][b]Mass turret-behaviour menu[/b][/size]

Open the right-click menu in space, on the map, or directly on a player-owned ship/station. The mod can add one of three sections, each shown only when relevant:

[list]
[*][b]Turret Behaviour[/b] — for the player's [i]occupied[/i] ship.[.*]
[*][b]Selected Turret Behaviour[/b] — for the ships in your current map selection.[.*]
[*][b]Station Turret Behaviour[/b] — for the player-owned station you clicked.[.*]
[/list]

The menu picks one scope at a time: selected ships first, then stations, then the player's occupied ship. Extension Options can hide any of these three sections; the player-ship section is off by default.

Each section has the same flat layout at root level (sizes the player doesn't own are hidden):

[code]
Turret Behaviour
├─ All                       — every turret on the target
├─ XL Turrets                — all XL turrets
├─ XL Turrets (per type)     — list of XL types → click any to drill in
├─ L Turrets                 — all L turrets
├─ L Turrets (per type)
├─ M Turrets                 — all M turrets
├─ M Turrets (per type)
├─ S Turrets                 — all S turrets
└─ S Turrets (per type)
[/code]

The "per type" entries use a nested navigation panel (chemodun's sub-group API from kuertee). Click into one to reach a specific turret type and apply behaviour to it alone.

Available actions inside every leaf section:

[list]
[*][b]Armed / Disarmed[/b][/*]
[*][b]Attack all enemies[/b][/*]
[*][b]Attack only capital ships[/b] / [b]Attack capital ships first[/b][/*]
[*][b]Attack only fighters[/b] / [b]Attack fighters first[/b][/*]
[*][b]Defend[/b][/*]
[*][b]Mining[/b] [i](ships only)[/i][/*]
[*][b]Missile defence[/b] / [b]Shoot missiles first[/b][/*]
[*][b]Attack my current enemy[/b] [i](ships only)[/i][/*]
[*][b]Towing[/b] [i](only on per-type entries for tug-class turrets)[/i][/*]
[/list]

The station section additionally exposes [b]All Missile Turrets[/b] / [b]All Non-Missile Turrets[/b] alongside the size buckets.

[size=3][b]Picking the right scope[/b][/size]

The intent is: pick the smallest scope that does what you need.

[list]
[*][i]Whole fleet, blanket order[/i]: top-level [b]All[/b] → done.[/*]
[*][i]One size class across a fleet[/i]: [b]L Turrets[/b] / [b]M Turrets[/b] → done.[/*]
[*][i]Specific turret model, e.g. all "L Beam Mk1"[/i]: open [b]L Turrets (per type)[/b] → click [b]L Beam Mk1[/b] → pick the mode.[/*]
[/list]

This keeps the menu navigable even with very large fleet selections, where the per-type list would otherwise scroll off the screen.

[line]

[size=4][b]Copy turret behaviour from one ship to others[/b][/size]

When you right-click a [b]player-owned ship[/b] while you have [b]at least one other player ship selected[/b] in the map, two extra entries appear in the [i]Orders for ...[/i] section:

[list]
[*][b]Copy Turret Behaviour (by type)[/b][/*]
[*][b]Copy Turret Behaviour (by slot)[/b][/*]
[/list]

Both copy the [i]full[/i] behaviour (mode + armed/disarmed) of the right-clicked ship's turrets onto every other ship in the current selection. The right-clicked ship itself is excluded. If the right-clicked ship has no turrets, the operation is skipped silently.

The two modes differ in how they decide [i]which[/i] config from the source maps to [i]which[/i] turret on each destination ship.

Both algorithms share the same fallback chain — they only differ in their first tier. The shared tail is, in order:

[list=1]
[*][b]Same firing style at same size[/b]: source's first turret of the same size whose firing-style icon (the small icon on each turret card in the equipment UI) matches the destination's. Two turrets share a firing style when the game UI renders the same icon for them, so e.g. Plasma↔Plasma, Bolt↔Bolt, Beam↔Beam, Tracking↔Tracking. This means a destination M Bolt Mk1 with no source M Bolt will still pick up a source M Pulse Mk1's config only if Pulse and Bolt share an icon (they don't), but will gladly pick up another M Bolt variant when available.[/*]
[*][b]Same size, any firing style[/b]: source's first turret of the same size.[/*]
[*][b]Fallback[/b]: source's very first turret, period.[/*]
[/list]

[size=3][b]Algorithm — by type[/b][/size]

The first tier:

[list]
[*]Take the destination turret's [font=Courier New](size, type)[/font] — e.g. [font=Courier New](M, Plasma Mk1)[/font].[/*]
[*]If the source ship has a turret with exactly the same [font=Courier New](size, type)[/font], apply its mode + armed state.[/*]
[/list]

Then fall through the shared chain above.

Use this mode when copying across [b]different ship hulls[/b] — the source and destination loadouts don't have to line up. Anything the source has equipped of the same type gets matched first; anything else is matched by firing style then by size.

[size=3][b]Algorithm — by slot[/b][/size]

The first tier:

[list]
[*]Take the destination turret's [b]slot index[/b] (for singular hull slots) or its [font=Courier New](path, group)[/font] identifier (for grouped turrets on capital ships).[/*]
[*]If the source has a turret at the same identifier [i]and[/i] the sizes line up, apply its config.[/*]
[/list]

Then fall through the shared chain above.

Use this mode when copying across [b]same-hull ships[/b] with potentially [i]different per-slot configuration[/i] — e.g. you've tuned a Behemoth so that M1–M3 are [i]Defend[/i] and M4–M6 are [i]Attack fighters first[/i], and you want every other Behemoth in the fleet to mirror exactly that layout, position by position.

The "by type" mode would collapse all those M Pulse Mk1 turrets to one common behaviour. The "by slot" mode preserves the position-specific tuning.

[size=3][b]Choosing between the two[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Different ship classes / loadouts[/b] in the selection → use [b]by type[/b].[/*]
[*][b]Same hull, same equipped types, blanket per-type behaviour[/b] → use [b]by type[/b].[/*]
[*][b]Same hull, per-position tuning that varies between slots[/b] → use [b]by slot[/b].[/*]
[/list]

When in doubt, [b]by type[/b] is the safer default. [b]By slot[/b] only beats it when slot position carries meaning the source ship has set up deliberately.

[size=3][b]Notes and caveats[/b][/size]

[list]
[*]Mode and armed state are both copied.[/*]
[*]Stations are not valid copy sources or destinations from these entries; use the station section of the right-click menu for station turrets.[/*]
[*]No undo. Pick carefully; or use the menu to reapply.[/*]
[*]Extension Options can hide the selected-ships, station, and player-ship menu sections. Debug logging is also available there and is off by default.[/*]
[/list]

